          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A more general concept of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
          %p
            Disambiguation note: This article is titled
            %em Booij premetric space
            solely for disambiguation purposes as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/premetric+space"} premetric space
            is already used for various other generalizations of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Auke+Booij"} Auke Booij
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Booij20"} Booij 2020
            = succeed "." do
              %em premetric spaces
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong premetric space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with a ternary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim_\epsilon b
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \in \mathbb{Q}_+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_+
            represent the positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
          %p
            Premetric spaces are used as in the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book+real+numbers"} HoTT book real numbers
          %h3#real_premetric_spaces Real premetric spaces
          %p
            The usual notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            rather than the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            in the metric inequalities. This means that to generalize from metric spaces, one can use the positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            instead of the positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            as the indexing set of the ternary relation (i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim_\epsilon b
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in S
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \in \mathbb{R}_+
            = succeed "." do
              %em real premetric space
            = succeed "." do
              %em rational premetric space
          %h3#uniform_premetric_spaces Uniform premetric spaces
          %p
            A rational or real premetric space is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+space"} uniform
            if
          %ol
            %li
              %p
